Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether every tempered distribution $u \in \mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is bounded by some Schwartz seminorm $\|\cdot\|_m$.

**Answer: Yes**, such $m$ and $C$ exist for every $u \in \mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^n)$.

## Proof

**Setup.** The Schwartz space $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a Fréchet space whose topology is generated by the countable separating family of seminorms $\{\|\cdot\|_m\}_{m=0}^{\infty}$, where
$$\|\phi\|_m = \sum_{|\alpha|+|\beta|\le m}\sup_{x\in\mathbb{R}^n}|x^\alpha\partial_x^\beta\phi(x)|.$$
A neighborhood base at the origin is given by the sets
$$V_{m,\epsilon} = \{\phi \in \mathcal{S} : \|\phi\|_m < \epsilon\}, \qquad m\ge 0,\;\epsilon > 0.$$

The space $\mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is **defined** as the continuous dual of $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$, i.e., the space of all continuous linear functionals on $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$.

**Key claim.** For a locally convex space whose topology is generated by seminorms $\{p_k\}_{k=0}^\infty$, a linear functional $u$ is continuous if and only if there exist $C>0$ and an index $m\ge 0$ such that $|u(\phi)| \le C\, p_m(\phi)$ for all $\phi$.

**Proof of the claim.**

*($\Leftarrow$) Bounded $\Rightarrow$ continuous.* If $|u(\phi)| \le C\|\phi\|_m$ for all $\phi$, then $u^{-1}((-1,1)) \supseteq V_{m, 1/C}$, so $u$ is continuous at the origin, hence continuous (being linear).

*($\Rightarrow$) Continuous $\Rightarrow$ bounded.* Suppose $u$ is continuous. Then $u^{-1}((-1,1))$ is an open neighborhood of the origin. Since $\{V_{m,\epsilon}\}$ forms a neighborhood base, there exist $m\ge 0$ and $\epsilon > 0$ such that
$$V_{m,\epsilon} \subseteq u^{-1}((-1,1)),$$
i.e., $\|\phi\|_m < \epsilon \implies |u(\phi)| < 1$.

Now take any $\phi \in \mathcal{S}$ with $\phi \neq 0$. Set $\psi = \dfrac{\epsilon\,\phi}{2\|\phi\|_m}$, so that $\|\psi\|_m = \epsilon/2 < \epsilon$, hence $|u(\psi)| < 1$. By linearity,
$$|u(\psi)| = \frac{\epsilon}{2\|\phi\|_m}|u(\phi)| < 1 \implies |u(\phi)| < \frac{2}{\epsilon}\|\phi\|_m.$$
Setting $C = 2/\epsilon$ gives $|u(\phi)| \le C\|\phi\|_m$ for all $\phi$ (the case $\phi = 0$ is trivial).

**Conclusion.** Since $u \in \mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is by definition a continuous linear functional on $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$, applying the claim with $p_m = \|\cdot\|_m$ yields: there exist an integer $m \ge 0$ and a constant $C > 0$ such that
$$|u(\phi)| \le C\|\phi\|_m \quad \text{for all } \phi \in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n).$$

This is precisely the characterization of tempered distributions: $\mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^n)$ consists exactly of those linear functionals on $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ that are bounded by a single Schwartz seminorm $\|\cdot\|_m$ for some $m$. $\blacksquare$

